        "title": "A disaster in the making",
        "text": "Most scientists agree that global warming is the greatest threat to the environment. There is little doubt that the Earth is getting warmer: in the last century the average temperature around the world went up by about 0.6 degrees C. The effects of global warming are often clear, from the melting of the ice cap on Mount Kilimanjaro, Africa's highest mountain, to the loss of coral reefs as oceans become warmer. Many experts warn that global warming will make sea levels rise dramatically. In the past 100 years the oceans have risen by 10 to 20 cm, but that is nothing compared with what would happen if, for example, Greenland's massive ice sheet were to melt. 'The consequences would be catastrophic,' says Jonathan Overpeck, Director of the Institute for the Study of Planet Earth at the University of Arizona. 'Even with a small rise in sea level, we're going to destroy a number of nations and cultures that have existed for thousands of years.' Overpeck and his colleagues have used computer models to produce a series of maps showing the places most at risk of flooding.\n\nJust as the evidence is clear that temperatures have risen in the last century, it is also well established that carbon dioxide in the Earth's atmosphere has increased by about 30 per cent, allowing the atmosphere to trap too much heat. However, the exact link, if there is one, between the rise in carbon dioxide emissions and the higher temperatures is still being argued about. Most scientists believe that humans, by burning fossil fuels such as coal and petroleum, are largely responsible for the increase in carbon dioxide. But some scientists also point to natural causes, such as volcanic activity. 'There are many uncertainties about global warming,' says Ronald Stouffer at the US National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration's Geophysical Fluid Dynamics Laboratory. 'How much of it would still happen if humans were not changing the climate in any way?'\n\nHowever, the present rate of warming is faster than ever before, which suggests that it is probably not a natural event. And a large number of scientists believe that the rise in temperatures will, in fact, speed up. In 2001 the UN Group on Climate Change reported that the average temperature is likely to rise by between 1.4 and 5.8 degrees C by the year 2100. The change in climate is likely to affect ecosystems, agriculture and the spread of disease. 'Global warming is a serious threat to animal and plant life,' says Jay Malcolm, a forestry professor at the University of Toronto. 'As climates warm, more southerly species will start to appear further north … species will find themselves in habitats where they don't belong. For example, glaciers and sea ice in both the northern and southern hemispheres are already melting fast, which puts animals like polar bears at risk.'\n\nA recent study suggested that Greenland's ice sheet will begin to melt if the temperature there rises by three degrees C, which many scientists think is likely to happen in another hundred years. If the Greenland ice cap melted completely, sea levels would rise by seven metres. Even partial melting would cause a one-metre rise. Such a rise would have a devastating effect on low-lying islands such as the Maldives, which would disappear under the sea completely. Densely populated areas like the Nile Delta and parts of Bangladesh would become impossible to live in, affecting hundreds of millions of people. A one-metre rise in sea level would also flood the eastern coast of the USA.\n\nOther scientists stress that such doomsday scenarios may be hundreds of years in the future. 'You can't say with any certainty that sea-level rises are going to have a huge impact on society,' says Stouffer. 'Who knows what the planet will look like 5,000 years from now?' Most climate scientists, however, agree that global warming is a threat that has been allowed to continue unchecked for too long. 'Is society aware of how serious climate warming is? I don't think so,' says Marianne Douglas, professor of geology at the University of Toronto. 'Otherwise we'd all be living our lives differently. We'd see a society that used alternative sources of energy and depended less on fossil fuels.'"
